WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.716
Start and controls
Take a seat and press Ready. The host starts when everyone is ready. Your cards or dice are at the bottom.

00:00:13.716 --> 00:00:20.016
Follow the turn prompt. Wait for animations to finish; an expired turn is played automatically. Use Return to room after settlement.

00:00:20.016 --> 00:00:30.015
Banqi: all 32 pieces start covered
We keep the first mover’s perspective. Neither player knows the covered identities, and neither has a colour yet.

00:00:30.015 --> 00:00:42.014
Goal: capture all enemies or block their actions
On your turn, flip a covered piece or move one of your face-up pieces. The first flipped colour determines both players’ colours.

00:00:42.014 --> 00:01:00.014
Choose a covered piece to flip
Click to flip with a mouse. Touch previews before Confirm or a second tap. The identity becomes public only after the flip.

00:01:00.014 --> 00:01:16.014
The first flip is a black general: we are black
Our first flip revealed the black general, making us black and the opponent red. A player can flip an opposing colour but cannot move it.

00:01:16.014 --> 00:01:27.014
Chain mode also allows blind attacks
Our black general can attack an adjacent covered piece without knowing its identity. Select the face-up attacker, then the legal covered target.

00:01:27.014 --> 00:01:36.014
This match uses the default chain and blind captures, with Rook dash off. The target is revealed and resolved only after the attack.

00:01:36.014 --> 00:01:44.814
Failed blind attack: reveal and end the turn
The target was our own black soldier. The general stayed put, the soldier turned face up, and the opponent now acts. Covered identities cannot be inspected first.

00:01:44.814 --> 00:01:52.013
A capturable enemy is taken and entered. A friendly or uncatchable piece is only revealed, ending the turn.

00:01:52.013 --> 00:02:08.013
Choose a face-up enemy to capture
The opponent just revealed a red elephant that our black general can take. Select the general, then the legal target; touch normally asks for confirmation.

00:02:08.013 --> 00:02:22.013
Use End turn to decline another capture
The capture left a chain available, so the turn has not passed. End turn gives up the remaining capture opportunity.

00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:33.013
Faster play: flips and captures
The order is general, advisor, elephant, rook, horse, cannon, soldier. Normal movement is one orthogonal cell; equal or lower enemies can be captured.

00:02:33.013 --> 00:02:42.013
Soldiers can capture generals, but generals cannot capture soldiers. A cannon capture jumps exactly one piece in a straight line, regardless of target rank.

00:02:42.013 --> 00:03:06.013
Fast-forward through reveals and exchanges
Every flip, blind attack, move and End turn is retained as the board becomes public and the remaining pieces fight.

00:03:06.013 --> 00:03:15.913
Continue capturing with the same piece
Our black advisor just captured and can continue. Choose another legal target with that advisor, or use End turn.

00:03:15.913 --> 00:03:24.013
A chain cannot switch pieces or make a quiet move. A failed blind attack ends the turn immediately.

00:03:24.013 --> 00:03:33.913
Faster endgame: watch the quiet-move count
Both sides move their remaining pieces. A side behind in numbers can still capture, but must avoid losing all legal actions.

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:42.014
Fifty consecutive non-capturing moves draw. Flips and blind attacks reset the count. A draw offer needs the opponent’s agreement.

00:03:42.014 --> 00:03:54.014
Our last black piece is threatened
We have one black advisor left, beside a red advisor. Watch the final capture and the server’s result.

00:03:54.014 --> 00:04:11.000
Result: red captures every black piece
Our black pieces are gone; red wins with eight left. The complete match has 81 flip/move entries and 12 End turn actions. Review the result and return to the room.
